const D={"vocab": [{"u": 1, "t": "Managers", "d": "A manager is an individual who is in charge of a department and ensures work efficiency through planning, organizing, controlling and commanding resources.", "e": "Lan is the sales manager. She manages 8 salespeople. Every morning, she assigns tasks, checks sales results, and helps employees when they have problems.", "n": "Ad: A manager ensures stability, clear organization and high efficiency in daily operations through clear rules and procedures The main difference is that managers have subordinates who obey them because of their job title and formal authority, whereas leades have followers who follow them voluntarily out of trust and inspiration. Managers focus on maintaining order and system while leaders focus on driving change and vision Dis: A manager can be overly strict and control-oriented, whcih may limit employee creativity and innovation", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 1, "t": "Leaders", "d": "A leader is an individual who creates a vision and inspires people to work together through personal qualities rather than formal power.", "e": "Steve Jobs was a leader at Aoole who inspired his team with a shared vision to challenge the status quo and create revolutionary products.", "n": "Ad: A leader builds strong trust. motivates employees deeply and promotes business innovation Dis: A leader may lack focus on daily routine details, administrative procedures and system controls.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 1, "t": "Planning", "d": "The process of deciding what to do and how to do it.", "e": "ABC Company wants to increase sales by 20% next year. The manager decides to hire 5 more salespeople, advertise on TikTok, and enter a new market. This is planning.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 1, "t": "Organizing", "d": "The process of arranging people and resources to work together.", "e": "After making the plan, the manager gives Lan responsibility for online sales, Minh responsibility for advertising, and An responsibility for customer service. This is organizing.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 1, "t": "Controlling", "d": "The process of checking work and fixing problems.", "e": "At the end of the month, the manager checks the sales report. The target was ₫500 million, but actual sales were only ₫400 million. She finds that online sales are too low and changes the advertising strategy.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 1, "t": "Coordinating", "d": "Making people or activities work well together.", "e": "The sales manager is coordinating the sales and marketing teams. She tells the marketing team when to start the advertising campaign and tells the sales team when to contact customers.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 1, "t": "Commanding", "d": "Giving orders and telling people what to do.", "e": "The manager is commanding the employees. He gives them clear orders and expects them to follow his instructions.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Job satisfaction", "d": "Job satisfaction means the positive feeling a worker gets when they enjoy their job and feel happy with their work.", "e": "For example, a teacher feels job satisfaction when her students pass an exam and she feels that her work has made a positive difference.", "n": "Higher job satisfaction increases employee commitment and helps workers perform better. However, low job satisfaction can lead to higher employee turnover and more frequent absenteeism.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Job rotation", "d": "Job rotation means regularly moving employees between different tasks to reduce boredom and help them learn new skills.", "e": "For example, a bank clerk moves between cash handling and customer service so that they do not perform the same task every day.", "n": "Job rotation reduces boredom from doing the same repetitive task and helps employees learn different basic skills. However, moving between several simple tasks may still leave employees unmotivated in the long term. It changes what task the employee do, but does not increase task complexity or decision-making power", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Job enlargement", "d": "Job enlargement means adding more tasks at the same level to make a job more varied.", "e": "for example, a sales epmloyee at first just need to find new customers. Later, the manager ask she to answer the customers questions and prepare sales report.", "n": "Job enlargement makes daily work more interesting by adding different tasks to a job. However, adding more routine tasks without extra pay or recognition may make employees feel overworked and unrewarded. It increase task quantity to make work less repetitive, but do not increase authority", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Job enrichment", "d": "Job enrichment means adding higher-level tasks that give employees more skill, responsibility, and decision-making power.", "e": "For example, a sales officer at first just do the task that the manager gives, know she can decide contract prices directly within a certain limit without asking a manager for approval each time.", "n": "Job enrichment can increase employee motivation, achievement, and recognition by giving employees more responsibility. However, it requires employees to have or learn higher-level skills, which may cause stress if they do not receive enough support or training. It expands the job by giving the employees greater responisibility and decision-marking power", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Theory X", "d": "Theory X is a management theory that assumes workers dislike work, avoid responsibility, and need close supervision.", "e": "Theory X Case Study (Foxconn Electronics): At Foxconn factories, managers closely supervise workers and set strict daily production targets. They regularly check employees’ work to make sure everyone follows the rules and produces the required number of products.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Theory Y", "d": "Theory Y is a management theory that assumes workers enjoy work, are responsible, and can motivate themselves.", "e": "Theory Y Case Study (Google): Google gives software engineers flexible working hours and freedom to choose how they work. The company trusts employees to take responsibility, develop new ideas, and solve problems by themselves.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Hygiene factors", "d": "Hygiene factors are basic workplace conditions, such as salary, job security, and working conditions, that prevent employees from becoming dissatisfied.", "e": "Hygiene Factors / Satisfiers Case Study (Amazon): At Amazon warehouses, employees receive regular wages, health benefits, and safe working conditions. These basic benefits help prevent complaints and dissatisfaction, but they do not always make employees more motivated to work harder.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Satisfiers", "d": "Satisfiers are basic needs, such as salary, job security, and good working conditions, that prevent employees from complaining about their jobs.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 2, "t": "Motivators", "d": "Motivators are job-related factors, such as challenging work, recognition, responsibility, and promotion, that encourage employees to perform better.", "e": "Motivators Case Study (Microsoft): Microsoft gives developers challenging projects, recognition for good work, and opportunities to develop their careers. These factors can make employees feel successful and encourage them to work harder and develop new skills.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "Inhouse", "d": "A company uses its own employees to do a job instead of hiring another company.", "e": "Procter & Gamble (P&G) uses its own in-house scientists and internal research laboratories to develop new product formulas and clean technology.", "n": "Managers can directly supervise employees and control the work more easily. However, hiring and training employees takes time and money, and the company still has to pay employees when there is less work.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "Outsourcing", "d": "A company hires another company to do a job instead of using its own employees.", "e": "Apple outsources the physical manufacturing and assembly of iPhones to external suppliers like Foxconn, while keeping product design and software engineering strictly in-house.", "n": "The company can reduce the cost of hiring and paying full-time employees and can use outside specialists. However, communication problems or delays may occur when working with the external company.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "Team", "d": "A team is a group of employees who work together to complete a project or achieve a common goal.", "e": "Spotify organizes its workforce into autonomous, cross-functional teams called “Squads”. Each squad combines software developers, graphic designers, and product testers who have the freedom to design, test, and release specific app features independently.", "n": "A team structure can increase flexibility and speed up project work. It can also improve teamwork, creativity, and relationships among workers. However, working in teams can create a competitive environment where several teams have to compete with each other to complete a project.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "chain of command/hierachical or pyramidal, line structure", "d": "A hierarchical structure is an organizational model where managers have different levels of authority, and instructions usually move from senior managers to lower-level employees.", "e": "Toyota operates under a clear hierarchical chain of command in its manufacturing operations. Strategic plans flow from the President and Board of Directors through Plant Managers, Department Heads, and Section Supervisors to assembly line workers who follow exact operational standards.", "n": "It provides a clear line of authority, so every employee knows their direct manager and job duties. However, communication can be slower, decision-making can take more time, and too many management levels may reduce employee initiative and creativity.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "functional structure", "d": "A functional structure is an organizational model where a company divides employees into departments based on their main functions, such as finance, marketing, production, sales, and human resources.", "e": "Apple uses a functional organizational structure. Employees are grouped by specialized areas such as Hardware Engineering, Software Development, Design, and Marketing.", "n": "It helps employees develop specialized skills and improves efficiency because people with similar skills work together. However, employees may focus more on their own department than on the company as a whole, and communication problems can occur between departments.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "Flattening hierachies", "d": "Flattening hierarchies means reducing the number of management levels in a company and giving lower-level employees more decision-making power.", "e": "Tesla has reduced some layers of management and encouraged employees and engineers to communicate more directly with senior leadership to solve urgent manufacturing problems.", "n": "It can reduce administrative costs and make communication and decision-making faster. However, employees may feel stressed or confused if there is not enough guidance, and there may be fewer opportunities for traditional management promotion.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Reactive culture", "d": "A culture where people prefer to listen carefully, avoid direct conflict, respect authority, and keep good relationships instead of speaking quickly or directly.", "e": "A Japanese company may spend a lot of time listening to employees and customers before making a decision. During a meeting, employees may avoid directly saying that a manager’s idea is wrong because they want to respect the manager and keep a good relationship.", "n": "It can increase excellent team harmony and careful listenning, but decision - making can be slow. This culture helps people build trust and maintain good relationships because they listen carefully and avoid direct conflict. However, decisions may take more time, and problems may not be discussed openly.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Linear Active Culture", "d": "A culture where people are logical, organized, plan their work carefully, and usually prefer to do one task at a time.", "e": "A German company may make a clear work plan before starting a project. Employees usually follow the schedule, finish one task before moving to the next, and use facts and logic when making decisions.", "n": "It increases punctuality and operational efficiency, but lacks flexibility during sudden changes. This culture makes work organized and helps employees follow clear plans and deadlines. However, it can be less flexible when unexpected problems happen or when employees need to change plans quickly.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Multi-active Culture", "d": "A culture where people care about feelings and relationships and are comfortable doing several things at the same time.", "e": "A company in Italy may have a meeting where employees discuss several topics at the same time. People may interrupt each other, show their emotions, and focus on personal relationships while discussing business problems.", "n": "It builds strong relationship and high adaptability, but can cause schedule delays This culture can make communication more friendly and help people build strong personal relationships. However, doing many things at the same time can make work less organized and may cause some tasks to be forgotten.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Glocalization", "d": "The process of selling a product around the world while changing it to match the tastes, needs, and culture of local customers.", "e": "McDonald’s sells products around the world but changes its menu for local customers. In India, for example, McDonald’s offers more vegetarian products because many Indian customers do not eat beef.", "n": "It increases local customer acceptance and builds strong brand loyalty in foreign markets. but it also increases product development, marketing and supply chain costs. Glocalization helps companies attract local customers because products fit local tastes and cultures better. However, changing products for different countries can increase costs and make the business more difficult to manage.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Universalist", "d": "A culture where people believe that the same rules, laws, and contracts should apply to everyone, even when they have personal relationships.", "e": "A German company may use the same contract rules for all employees and business partners. Even if a manager has a close personal relationship with someone, the company still expects the same rules and procedures to be followed.", "n": "It ensures legal fariness and business transparency, but can appear cold or rigid. This culture makes rules and decisions more consistent because everyone follows the same standards. However, it can be difficult to deal with special situations when following the same rule is not the best solution.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Collectivelist", "d": "Collectivist is a culture where people prioritize team goals and group harmony rather than their own personal goals.", "e": "A Vietnamese company may organize team activities and encourage its workers to work together. Therefore, it seems like group performance is more important than individual performance.", "n": "The collectivist culture can increase loyalty and improve teamwork among employees, but it can also decrease independence and the ability to create new personal ideas. This culture encourages teamwork, loyalty, and support between employees. However, employees may have less freedom to make individual decisions or focus on their own goals.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Particularist", "d": "A culture where personal relationships and special situations can be more important than general rules.", "e": "Particularism is a culture where people prioritize personal relationships, and sometimes, special situations can be more important than general rules. For example, in China, many companies hire new employees through recommendations from current workers rather than through official job postings online to find suitable workers.", "n": "Particularist culture may increase trust between businesses and workers, but it also creates a high risk regarding the actual quality of the workers and products that are recommended. This culture allows people to consider personal relationships and special situations when making decisions. However, decisions may sometimes seem unfair because different people may receive different treatment.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Individualist", "d": "An individualist is a person who prioritizes their personal goals rather than the team's goals. They tend to work harder for themselves and focus on achieving their own goals.", "e": "In the United States, employees may be encouraged to develop their own careers and achieve personal goals. A salesperson may focus on reaching their own sales target and receiving an individual bonus.", "n": "It encourages personal intiative and ffast individual innovation but can reduce team unity. This culture encourages independence, personal responsibility, and individual achievement. However, employees may focus too much on their own goals and may cooperate less with other team members.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Power of distance", "d": "The degree to which people accept that some people in society or organizations have much more power than others.", "e": "In a company, power distance can be seen in how employees communicate with managers. If employees rarely question managers and managers make most important decisions, the company may have a higher level of power distance.", "n": "Understanding power distance helps managers choose the right way to communicate with employees from different cultures. However, a misunderstanding of power distance can create communication problems between managers and employees. | A high-power-distance culture is a culture where lower-level employees accept that they should obey their managers. | In contrast, low-power distance means that employees see managers as more equal, feel comfortable sharing their own opinions, and take part in decision-making.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "High context culture", "d": "High-context culture is a culture where people prefer to communicate through facial expressions, body language, and indirect communication in order to avoid situations where harsh words can hurt others.", "e": "In Asian countries, especially Japan, people prioritize keeping conversations gentle and friendly in order to show respect for others.", "n": "High-context protects personal feelings and maintans smooth harmony, but can cause misunderstandings for foreigners. This culture helps people communicate politely and protect relationships, especially when discussing sensitive topics. However, messages may be misunderstood because people do not always say exactly what they mean.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "Low context culture", "d": "Private recruitment happens when a company pays a third-party recruitment agency to hire employees for the company.", "e": "For example, in European countries, people tend to follow a low-context culture, where communication is expected to be clear and direct.", "n": "Low-context culture can help conversations become clearer and more direct, which means that it can prevent situations where people misunderstand each other because of unclear communication. But in Asian cultures, speaking too directly can be seen as disrespectful to others. This culture makes communication clear and reduces the need to guess the meaning of a message. However, very direct communication may sometimes sound too strong or unfriendly in cultures that prefer indirect communication.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Affirmative recruitment", "d": "Hiring people fairly and giving everyone an equal chance to get a job, regardless of their sex, skin color, religion, or disability.", "e": "Global technology companies such as IBM and Google use affirmative recruitment by actively looking for female engineers and people from underrepresented groups. This helps make sure that job opportunities are available to people from different backgrounds.", "n": "Affirmative recruitment promotes equal opportunities, creates a more diverse workforce, and helps companies avoid discrimination problems. However, some employees may feel that hiring decisions are not based only on a candidate’s skills and qualifications.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Private recruitment", "d": "Private recruitment happens when a company pays a third-party recruitment agency to hire employees for the company.", "e": "A large company may hire a recruitment agency such as Heidrick & Struggles to find senior managers or other highly skilled workers. The agency searches for candidates, checks their qualifications, and helps the company choose a suitable person.", "n": "Private recruitment saves the HR department time and helps the company find skilled candidates more easily. However, the company has to pay the recruitment agency, so it can be expensive.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Induction", "d": "Induction is a training program for new employees that helps them understand the company's workplace and become more familiar with their colleagues and jobs..", "e": "For example, at McDonald's, new restaurant employees may have a one-day training session with experienced workers in order to become more familiar with their workload and the daily tasks they have to complete.", "n": "Induction helps new employees understand their job and feel more comfortable in their new workplace. It also helps them learn important safety rules. However, it takes time and money, and the new employee cannot do their normal work during the training.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "On the job training", "d": "On-the-job training is training that helps new employees learn their jobs by watching and working with experienced employees.", "e": "On-the-job training is training that helps new employees learn their jobs by watching and working with experienced employees.", "n": "On-the-job training is practical and usually costs less because employees learn while working. However, the new employee may learn bad habits from the trainer, and the experienced employee may work more slowly because they have to spend time training the new worker.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Off the job trainning", "d": "Off-the-job training is training that takes place outside the workplace. During the training course, employees are sent to a classroom or a training centre to learn about their jobs and develop their skills.", "e": "For example, Singapore Airlines sends its pilots and flight attendants to a training centre in order to help them learn about flight procedures and safety procedures", "n": "Off-the-job training allows employees to learn from professional trainers and practice difficult situations in a safe environment. However, it can be expensive because the company has to pay for training and travel, and employees are away from their normal work during the training.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Headhunter", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Employee selection", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Job analysis", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Job descriptition", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Job specification", "d": "Document listing the qualifications and skills needed for a job", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Shortlisted cadidates", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Aptitude tests", "d": "Aptitude tests are tests that a company uses to measure a candidate's abilities and skills.", "e": "FPT Corporation, one of Vietnam's leading technology companies, requires applicants for software engineering positions to complete an aptitude test in order to assess their IQ and technical skills.", "n": "Aptitude tests are a popular way that many large companies use to assess applicants when they need to hire employees. Aptitude tests can show an applicant's real abilities better than a CV can, which helps employers identify and hire the right people for their company.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "In-tray exercises", "d": "An in-tray exercise is a test where new employees have to deal with different tasks and problems related to their job position.", "e": "In the Unilever Future Leaders Program, Unilever uses a digital in-tray test for its candidates. Candidates might have to solve several difficult situations, such as handling a customer complaint, and deal with challenging situations, such as supply chain delays.", "n": "In-tray exercises help employers understand how well candidates organize their work, manage their time, and solve problems in realistic work situations. However, these tests take a lot of time to prepare and evaluate. They can also make some candidates feel stressed because they have to complete many tasks under time pressure. Aptitude tests are usually formal tests that applicants can complete in a short time, while an in-tray exercise usually requires more time. Aptitude tests mainly assess applicants in a theoretical way, while an in-tray exercise measures their ability to solve real-life problems and deal with situations that might happen at work.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Increment", "d": "An increment is a regular increase in an employee's basic salary, usually awarded annually based on their performance or length of service.", "e": "For example, Viettel Group, one of Vietnam's largest telecommunications companies, may give employees annual salary increments based on their length of service and KPI performance.", "n": "A salary increment can encourage workers to perform better in their jobs and stay with the company for a long time.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "Probation", "d": "A probation period is a trial period that a company gives to a new employee to check their suitability for the job.", "e": "For example, at VinFast, new employees, especially engineers and marketing managers, may have to undergo a two-month probation period before signing a labor contract with the company. During this period, the company evaluates their professional skills, attitudes, and ability to fit into the company’s culture.", "n": "Probation helps the company avoid permanently hiring employees who are not suitable for the job, while also giving new employees time to learn about the work and adapt to the company. However, new employees may feel worried or insecure because they do not yet have a permanent contract.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "The primary sectors", "d": "The primary sector is the part of the economy that extracts natural resources from the Earth and produces raw materials, such as farming, fishing, mining, and oil extraction.", "e": "Saudi Aramco and Vinacomin are examples of companies in the primary sector because they extract natural resources such as crude oil, natural gas, and coal from the Earth to provide raw materials for other industries.", "n": "The primary sector provides important natural resources for people and other businesses to use. It can also bring a lot of money to a country. However, it depends heavily on the weather, natural resources can run out, and prices can change a lot. The primary sector is a part of the economy that extract the natural resources from the Earth and produces raw materials", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "The secondary sectors", "d": "the secondary sector is the part of the economy that uses raw materials from the primary sector ro manufacture products", "e": "VinFast and Foxconn are examples of companies in the secondary sector because they use materials such as metals, batteries, and electronic components to manufacture finished products such as electric vehicles and smartphones.", "n": "The secondary sector turns raw materials into finished products and creates many jobs in factories. However, companies need a lot of money to buy machines and build factories, and factories can create waste and pollution. the secondary sector is a part of the economy that uses raw materials from the primary sector ro manufacture products.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "The tertiary", "d": "The tertiary sector is the part of the economy that provides services to consumers and other businesses, such as retail, banking, hotels, and logistics.", "e": "for example, Walmart is a retail company in the tertiary sector that sells goods directly to consumers instead of making them", "n": "The tertiary sectors provides jobs, boost the local economy and does not need heavy machines to produce goods.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "The quaternary", "d": "The quaternay sector is a knowledge-based industry focused on information, research and technology", "e": "Google is in the quaternary sector because it develops software, it uses AL research and data analysis to provide knowledge services rather than making physical goods", "n": "The quaternary sector can make a lot of money from knowledge, technology, and new ideas. It also helps companies develop new technology and improve their products. However, it needs highly skilled workers who can be expensive to hire, and research projects can cost a lot of money without guaranteeing success.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "Sole traders", "d": "A business owned and run by one person. The owner gets all the profits and is responsible for all the business debts.", "e": "Bánh Mì Huỳnh Hoa: Bánh Mì Huỳnh Hoa is a well-known bánh mì shop in Ho Chi Minh City. The owner manages the shop's daily operations, including preparing products, managing employees, purchasing ingredients, and serving customers. The owner receives the profits from the business and is responsible for its business expenses and debts.", "n": "A sole trader is easy and cheap to start, and the owner has full control over the business and keeps all the profits. However, the owner has unlimited liability, so personal assets may be used to pay business debts. It can also be difficult to raise capital, and the owner has a heavy workload.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "Partnerships", "d": "A business owned and run by two or more people who share the profits, responsibilities, and debts.", "e": "For example, Baker MCKenzie is a global law company run as a partnership. In this company, senior lawyers own the business together, work for client together, split the profits at the and of the year", "n": "A partnership is easy to set up, and the partners can combine their money and skills. They can also share the work and make decisions together. However, general partners may have unlimited liability, and conflicts can occur between partners. The profits must also be shared among the partners.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "Private limited companies", "d": "Unlike public limited companies whose shares can be bought and sold freely on the stock market, the shares of private limited companies are owned by a small group of shareholders and are not available to the general public", "e": "for example, Mars, a business specializing in selling products such as M&amp;M, Snickers and pet food,..., is a private limited company whose shares belong to the founder's family.", "n": "A private limited company protects its shareholders through limited liability, so their personal assets are generally protected from business debts. The company can also keep its financial information relatively private. However, it cannot sell shares to the general public, and it is more complex to set up than a sole trader.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "Public limited companies", "d": "A public limited company is a type of business where a company sells its shares publicly on the stock market. Any investor who buys the shares becomes a shareholder in the company..", "e": "Apple is a public limited company whose shares are publicly traded on the stock market. Selling shares is also a way for the company to raise funds and capital.", "n": "A public limited company can raise a large amount of capital by selling shares to the public, and shareholders can easily buy and sell these shares. However, the company may face a hostile takeover, and it must follow strict legal requirements and publish financial information.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "Franchises", "d": "A franchise is a type of business in which people pay an amount of money to a company to use its brand name and business model. After becoming a franchisee, people can sell products under the brand name of the original well-known company and earn profits for themselves after paying fees to the franchisor..", "e": "McDonald's is typically a franchise business because it allows independent business owners to open restaurants using the McDonald's name, menu, equipment, and operating system. A franchisee can open a McDonald's restaurant and manage its employees and daily operations while following McDonald's standards.", "n": "A franchise model can benefit both the franchisee and the franchisor. By using the already well-known brand name of the original company, a franchisee can reduce its marketing costs and attract the company's loyal customers. A franchisor can increase its income without directly selling products to customers by charging franchisees fees for using its brand name.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "Joint Ventures", "d": "A joint venture is a type of business where two or more companies work together to create and develop a business project. For that project, they share the costs, profits, and risks.", "e": "Sony Ericsson: Sony and Ericsson combined their technology and telecommunications expertise to create Sony Ericsson in 2001. Sony contributed its consumer electronics and entertainment expertise, while Ericsson contributed its telecommunications technology. Together, they developed and sold mobile phones under the Sony Ericsson brand.", "n": "A joint venture allows companies to combine their skills and market knowledge while sharing the costs and profits. Developing a new project with another specialized company allows them to enter a new market with lower risks than entering the market alone. A joint venture is usually formed between two or more companies for a specific project, while a partnership is usually owned and run by two or more individuals who share the profits, responsibilities, and debts.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "Lean production", "d": "Lean production is a way of producing goods or services while using as little waste, time, and effort as possible.", "e": "Toyota uses lean production through its Toyota Production System (TPS). For example, Toyota uses Just-in-Time (JIT) to receive parts when they are needed, reducing excess inventory. It also uses Kaizen to continuously improve production processes and reduce waste.", "n": "Lean production reduces waste and lowers the cost of storing extra materials and products. It also helps the company work more efficiently. However, it can be easily affected by supply chain problems if a supplier cannot deliver materials on time. Lean production vs. Kaizen: Lean production is an overall way of managing production to reduce waste and improve efficiency. Kaizen is a specific practice of making small and continuous improvements to work.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "Kaizen", "d": "Kaizen is a Japanese idea of making small and regular improvements to work and reducing waste.", "e": "Canon and Honda encourage assembly line workers to suggest small changes every day, such as changing the position of tools to reduce unnecessary movement and reduce mistakes.", "n": "Kaizen does not usually require a lot of money because improvements come from employees’ ideas and small changes. However, it takes time and strong support from management, and employees may resist changes if the company culture is too rigid. Kaizen vs. Innovation: Kaizen focuses on making small improvements regularly, often based on employees’ ideas. Innovation usually involves creating something new or making a major change, often through new technology or new ideas.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "Cell production", "d": "Cell production is a production method where workers and machines are divided into small groups called cells, and each cell makes a specific part of the finished product.", "e": "Toyota uses a cell production by grouping workers and machines into small production cells. One cells can be responsible for assembling the car seats, while another cell ressembles the dashboard. Worker in each cells work together to complete their specific part before sending it to the next stage of production", "n": "Cell production can improve employee motivation because workers feel more responsible and valued when they work in small teams. However, employees may need extra training, and total production may be lower than with a continuous flow production line. Cell production vs. Flow production: Cell production divides workers and machines into small teams, with each team making a specific part or module of a product. Flow production uses a continuous production line where workers usually perform one specific task repeatedly.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "Job production", "d": "Job production is a production method where a product is made individually and specially for one customer’s order.", "e": "Rolls-Royce uses job production to manufacture luxury cars according to each customer's requirements. For example, a customer can choose the car's exterior color, interior materials, and special features. Each car is made to meet the customer's specific preferences.", "n": "Job production allows companies to make high-quality products that are exactly what each customer wants. However, making products individually takes more time and makes each product much more expensive to produce. Job production vs. Batch production: Job production makes one product at a time, usually according to one customer's specific requirements. Batch production makes a group of the same products together before moving to another product.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "Batch production", "d": "Batch production is a production method where a group of the same products is made together before moving to another product.", "e": "Toyota uses batch production by manufacturing a group of cars of the same model before switching to another model. For example, it may produce a batch of Toyota Corolla cars before moving to Toyota Camry cars.", "n": "Batch production allows a company to produce different types of products using the same machines. However, the company may need to store unfinished products, and production time can be lost when machines need to be cleaned or changed for a new batch. Batch production vs. Flow production: Batch production makes products in groups and may stop between batches to change or reset the machines. Flow production makes large quantities of products continuously with very little stopping.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "Flow production", "d": "Flow production is a production method where a company makes a lot of the same product continuously on a production line.", "e": "Coca-Cola and Foxconn use continuous production lines to make very large quantities of identical products, such as cans of soft drinks and electronic devices.", "n": "Flow production can produce a very large number of products quickly and at a low cost per product. However, setting up the machines is very expensive, and the production line is not flexible when the company wants to change the product design. Flow production vs. Job production: Flow production makes large quantities of standardized products continuously, so the cost per product is usually low. Job production makes individual products for specific customers, so the cost per product is usually higher.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "lead time", "d": "Lead time is the total time between placing an order and receiving the goods. It includes the time from starting the production process until the goods are completed and sent to the retailer or customer..", "e": "For example, Dell starts producing a laptop after receiving a customer's order. The lead time includes the time from starting production until the laptop is completed and delivered to the customer.", "n": "A short lead time can make customers more satisfied because they receive their orders sooner. It can also reduce the amount of money the company has tied up in inventory. However, reducing lead time may require more expensive transport or suppliers that are closer to the factory. Lead time vs. Delivery time: Lead time is the total time from when an order is placed until the order is completed and received. Delivery time usually refers to the time it takes for the product to travel from the seller or warehouse to the customer.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "delivery time", "d": "delivery time is the time it takes to deliver goods fromt he warehouse to the customer. it does not include the time needed to produce the goods", "e": "for example: Amazon uses large logistics networks and warehouses close to customers to reduce the delivery time between placing an order and receiving the product", "n": "A short delivery time can increase customer satisfaction and encourage customers to buy from the company again. However, it requires expensive warehouses, delivery systems, and technology to track orders. Delivery time vs. Lead time: Delivery time focuses mainly on how long it takes to transport a product to the customer. Lead time covers the whole process, from receiving the order to completing and delivering it.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "Push and pull stategies", "d": "Push strategy means producing goods before customers buy them, based on expected demand. Pull strategy means producing goods after customers place orders, based on actual demand.", "e": "Coca Cola uses push strategy as the producing large quanitities of soft drinks in advance based on sales forecasts for each season. In contrast, DELL uses pull strategy by producing laptops after the customer places order.", "n": "Push strategy can reduce production costs as companies can produce large quantities of goods at once. Besides, producing goods in advance can ensure the availability of products for customers. However, if the sales forecasts are inaccurate, this can lead to excess inventory. Pull strategy can decrease inventory costs as companies produce goods based on actual customer demand. However, when there is a sudden increase in demand, there can be stock shortages or delays. Push vs Pull: Push produces goods based on forecasts, while Pull produces goods based on actual customer demand.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "Inbound", "d": "Inbound logistics is the process of moving and storing raw materials, components, and supplies from suppliers to a company.", "e": "Coca-Cola uses inbound logistics by receiving raw materials such as sugar, water, and packaging materials from suppliers for its factories.", "n": "Inbound logistics helps a company receive materials on time and keep production running smoothly. However, supplier delays, transportation problems, or higher shipping costs can interrupt production. Inbound vs Outbound logistics: Inbound logistics moves raw materials and supplies into a company, while outbound logistics moves finished products out to customers.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "Outbound logistic", "d": "Outbound logistics is the process of storing, transporting, and delivering finished products from a company to customers or other businesses.", "e": "Coca-Cola manages outbound logistics by transporting finished drinks from its bottling plants to supermarkets, restaurants, and other retailers.", "n": "Outbound logistics helps companies deliver products quickly, satisfy customers, and reach more markets. However, transportation, warehouses, fuel, and delivery services can be expensive. Outbound vs Inbound logistics: Outbound logistics focuses on delivering finished products to customers, while inbound logistics focuses on bringing materials from suppliers to the company.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "Customs clearance", "d": "Customs clearance is the official process of getting permission from customs authorities to import or export goods and paying any required duties and taxes.", "e": "Nike handles customs clearance by preparing documents, checking product information, and completing customs procedures for international shipments.", "n": "Customs clearance helps companies follow import and export laws and avoid fines or delays. However, the process can involve complex documents, taxes, and long waiting times at borders. Customs clearance vs Freight forwarding: Customs clearance deals with documents, taxes, and legal requirements for crossing borders, while freight forwarding arranges the transportation of goods.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "Reverse logistics", "d": "Reverse logistics is the process of moving goods from customers back to a company for returns, repairs, recycling, reuse, or disposal.", "e": "Apple uses reverse logistics when customers return old iPhones for trade-in or recycling, allowing the company to reuse or recycle some of the materials.", "n": "Reverse logistics allows companies to recover useful materials, handle returns, and reduce waste. However, returning and processing products creates extra transportation and operating costs. Reverse logistics vs Forward logistics: Reverse logistics moves products from customers back to the company, while forward logistics moves products from the company to customers.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "reverse logistic is the process of moving goods from the customer back to the company because the goods is damage and need to repair", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 17, "t": "INTERNATIONAL TRADE", "d": "International trade is the exchange of goods and services between different countries through imports and exports", "e": "For example, VietNam exports coffee to Japan, Coffee is a product grown in VietNam, then deliveried to Japan for people to buy and drink. So this is called Internation trade.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "FREE TRADE", "d": "Free trade means countries can buy and sell goods and services with few or no restrictions, such as extra taxes on imported products.", "e": "For example, VietNam exports coffee to Japan without paying extra taxes on the coffee, this makes it easier and cheaper for Japan to buy coffee from VietNam. This is called free trade.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "PROTECTIONISM", "d": "Protectionism is when a country tries to protect its local industries from foreign competition by making it more difficult or more expensive to buy products from other countries", "e": "For example, in 2018, the United States introduced extra taxes on imported steel to protect its local steel industry, making imported steel more expensive. Therefore, US companies had more reasons to choose steel produced by local companies. This is called protectionism.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "TRADE BARRIERS", "d": "Trade barriers are rules, restrictions that make it more difficult and expensive to buy or sell products and services form other countries.", "e": "For example, the trade war between China and the United States began in 2018. the United Stated introduced an extra 25% taxes on products imported from China, including machinery and technology products. In response, China increased taxes on products imported from the US, such as soybeans.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "TARRIF", "d": "Tarrif is a tax that a government imposes to goods imported from other countries. It is also a type of trade barrier, as it increase the price of imported products", "e": "For example, the trade war between China and the United States began in 2018. the United Stated introduced an extra 25% taxes on products imported from China, including machinery and technology products. In response, China increased taxes on products imported from the US, such as soybeans.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "QUOTA", "d": "Quota is a limit set by a government on the amount or number of products that can be imported from other countries. It is also a type of trade barrier as it makes trade between countries become more difficult.", "e": "For example, the United States limits the amount of sugar imported from other countries in order to protect the local farmers.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "ABSOLUTE ADVANTAGE", "d": "Absolute advantage is when a country can produce products more efficiently than any other countries.", "e": "For example, VietNam can produce 100 bags of coffee a day, while Thailand can produce 60 bags of coffee a day, using the same amount of resources. This means VietNam has an absolute advantage over Thailand in coffee production", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "COMPARATIVE ADVANTAGE", "d": "Competitive advantage is when a country can produce a product more efficiently than it can produce other products.", "e": "For example, VietNam can produce 100 bags of coffee a day, while it can produce 60 bags of rice a day. So coffee production is a competitive advantage compared with rice production in VietNam.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "AN INFANT INDUSTRY", "d": "An infant industry is a new industry in a country that needs time and support to compete with well-established companies.", "e": "For example, the car industry is an infant industry in VietNam as it is new and still struggles to grow compared with imported cars.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "A STATEGIC INDUSTRY", "d": "A stategic industry is an industry that is very important to a country's economy and growth, so the government may protect and support it.", "e": "For example, the electricity industry is a stategic industry in VIetnam as electricity is essential for people's daily life. So the goverment supports the EVN (Vietnam Electricity) to ensure a stable electricity supply", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "COPYRIGHT", "d": "Copyright is a legal right that protects a person's original work from being copied or used without permission.", "e": "Nguyen Ngoc Tu is a Vietnamese author. She owns a copyright to every book she has written.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "DUMPING", "d": "Dumping is when a company sells a in another countries at a price lower than its normal value, often lower than the price it charges in its home country.", "e": "A company sells steel for 100 dollar per ton in China, but only 70 dollar per ton in VietNam in order to attract foreign customers and weaken the local steel industry in VietNam.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "GENERIC PRODUCT", "d": "Generic is common, general, not connected in to a specific brand or name. It depends on the context.", "e": "Generic product is a cheaper copy that is not marked with the producer's name", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "SUBSIDIZE", "d": "Subsidize is to give financial support to a business to reduce its costs.", "e": "For example, Vietnamese government subsidizes rice productions by gives financial support to help farmers maintain rice-growing land and reduce costs.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "TRADEMARK", "d": "Trademark is a name, a logo, a symbol that identifies a company's products.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "EMBARGO", "d": "Embargo is a government ban on trade with a country or on a specific product.", "e": "For example, the United States imposed a trade embargo on Cuba, restricting trade between the two countries for political reason.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "MULTINATIONAL CORPORATION (MNC)", "d": "Multinational corporation is a large company that operates in more than two countries.", "e": "For example, SamSung is a multinational corporation as it is based on South Korean and operates factories and businesses in other countries, including VietNam.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "AUTARKY", "d": "Autarky is when a country tries to produce all the products it needs but not rely on imported products.", "e": "For example, North Korea tries to achieve autarky by producing goods domestically and reducing its dependence on international trade.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "DEFICIT", "d": "Deficit is a situation where a country spends more money on imported products than it earns from exported products.", "e": "the United States has a trade deficit because the total value of its imports of goods is greater than the value of its exports of goods.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "SURPLUS", "d": "Surplus is a situation where a country earns more money from exported products than it spends on imported products.", "e": "China has a trade surplus becauses it has the total money earns from exported products higher than the money it spends on imported products.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "BALANCE OF PAYMENTS", "d": "Balance of payment is the record of all transactions between a country and another country.", "e": "For example, VietNam receives money when foreign tourists come and spend money in Vietnam.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "BALANCE OF TRADE", "d": "Balance of trade is the difference between the total value of goods a country exported and the total value of goods a country imported", "e": "China sells 100 000 dollar for foreign countries and spends 70 000 dollars, so China has a trade surplus.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "BARTER- COUNTERTRADE", "d": "Barter is a way of exchanging goods and services for other goods and services without using money", "e": "Pepsico agreed to sell Pepsi in the Sovit Union and received Sovit Vodka in return, which is sold in the United States", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "VISIBLE TRADE - MERCHANDISE TRADE", "d": "Visible trade is the international trade of physical goods such as clothes, rice, furnitures,...", "e": "VietNam exports coffee to Japan", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "JOB INSECURITIES", "d": "Job insecurity is the fear of losing your job.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "EMPLOYABILITY", "d": "Employability is the ability to get and keep a job because of having the right skills, knowledge and experience", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "DOWNSIZING", "d": "Downsizing is when a company reduces the number of workers to reduce costs.", "e": "Disney lays off 1000 employees to reduce costs.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "RIGHTSIZING", "d": "Rightsizing is when a company changes the number of workers to meet its needs", "e": "During Covid 19, many companies changed its workforce because they have fewer customers and lower sales", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "CONTACT WORK", "d": "Contract work is a job where the worker agree to work for a company for a specific period", "e": "For example, a company hires an IT worker for 6 months to develop a new website. The worker's contract ends after 6 months.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "CAUSUAL WORK", "d": "Casual work is work that does not have a regular working hours. Workers are usually hired when the employer need extra help", "e": "The restaurant hires extra workers on weekend as it has more customers. These workers only work on that weekend.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "RATIONNALIZATION", "d": "Rationalization is when a company changes the way it works to reduce costs and improve efficiency. Ratinalization may include downsizing, but it can improve the efficiency without cutting kobs.", "e": "in 2024, Unilever imporved its Factory in Germany by using machines to reduce food waste by 50%, saving over a million dollars.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "RESTRUCTURING", "d": "Restructuring is when a company changes it structure improve cooperation", "e": "Recently, Kraft Heinz, the company behind Heinz ketchup and Kraft Cheese announced that it would combines it purchasing and supply chain departments into one department to improve efficency", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "REDUNDANCY", "d": "Redundancy is when a company no longer needs a job so the worker loses their job", "e": "Recently, Microsoft laid off some employees in Vietnam as part of its workforce reductions in 2026", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "REDUCDANCY PACKAGE", "d": "Redundancy package is money and benefits that a company gives to its workers who lose their job because a company no longer needs their positions.", "e": "Microsoft laid off about 10 000 employees and offered affected workers severance pay and other benefits.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "JOB SHARING", "d": "Job sharing is when two workers share one full-time job and divide the working hours and the salary between them.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "COUNTRACTING - OUT", "d": "Contracting out (outsourcing) is when a company hires another company to do work or provide service for it", "e": "MC Donald's hires an outside cleanning company to clean its restaurants instead of employing its own cleaners.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "DELOCALIZAION - RELOCATION", "d": "Delocalization is when a company moves its production or business activities to another country , often to reduce costs. Relocation is moving into another place, which can in in the same country or abroad", "e": "Apple moves its Iphone production to China because production costs can be lower there", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "DELAYERING", "d": "Delayering is when a company removes one or more level of management to make decisions faster", "e": "In 2025, Microsoft reduced management layers by cutting some management positions so that more employees could reported directly to each manager", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "LAY OFF", "d": "Lay off is when a company tells workers to leave their jobs, usually to reduce costs.", "e": "Recently, Microsoft cut some jobs in VietNam as part of changes to its organization.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY (CSR)", "d": "Corporate social responsibility (CSR) is when a company takes responsibility for its environmental and social impacts, rather than only focusing on its profits.", "e": "Unilever is a famous example of Corporate Social Responsibility (RSC). The company promotes human rights in its supply chain and works to reduce its environmental impacts. In 2021, the company has works with VietCycle to provide trainning and protective equipment to more than 3000 waste collectors in VietNam.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "SHAREHOLDER", "d": "Shareholder is a person or a company that owns shares in a business.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "STOCKHOLDER MODEL", "d": "Stockholder model says that a company's main responsibility is to maximize profits and increase value for its shareholders.", "e": "Apple is a famous example of Stockholder model. In 2025, Apple bought back its own shares and paid money to its shareholders. These actions help shareholders get money from their investment.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "STAKEHOLDER MODEL", "d": "Stakeholder model says that a company should consider the interests of all groups affected by its activities, not only shareholders.", "e": "Unilever is a famous example of stakeholder model The company considers the interests of different groups, including employees, suppliers, customers and the environment. For example, it promotes fair wages in its supply chain and requires suppliers to respect human right and environmental standards.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "UNDEMOCRATIC ACTIONS", "d": "Undemocratic actions are actions that do not allow people to express their opinions or take part in decisions.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "GREENWASHING", "d": "Greenwashing is when a company makes people believe its products or business are environmentally friendly, even though this is not true.", "e": "H&M is an example of greenwashing. In 2022, the company promoted its Conscious clothing collections as a more sustainable choice. However, Dutch authorities found that some of its environmental claims are unclear. After that, H&M agreed to stop using these claims.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "BUSINESS CYCLE MODEL", "d": "Business cycle model is a model that shows the fluctuations in economic activity over time", "e": "During expansion, people spend more money and busineses might hire more workers. During contraction, people spend less money and businesses might reduce production.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "EXPANSION", "d": "expantion is a period when economic activity increase and the economy grow", "e": "During COVID 19, Amazon earned more money because there was a growing demand for online shopping. The company then expanded its operations to meet this demand.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "RECESSION", "d": "recession is a period when economic activity declines significantly, affecting businesses and workers.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "DEPRESSION/SLUMP", "d": "Depression is a very serve and long-lasting decline in economic activity", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "PEAK", "d": "A peak is the highest point of economic activity in the business cycle, before the economy begins to slow down.", "e": "US housing market reached a peak in 2006.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "TROUGH", "d": "A trough is the lowest point of economic activity in the business cycle, before the economy begins to recover", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "CONTRACTION", "d": "contraction is a period when economic activity decreases and the economy slows down.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "RECOVERY", "d": "Recovery is a period when economic activity starts to increase again after a downturn", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "GROSS DOMESTIC PRODUCT (GDP)", "d": "Gross domestic product is the total value of final goods and services within a country during a specific period", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "DOWNTURN-UPTURN", "d": "downturn is a period when economic activity decreases while upturn is a period when economic activity increases after a period of decline.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "EQUILIBRIUM", "d": "Equilibrium is a situation where supply and demand are balanced, so the market price tends to remain stable.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "FISCAL POLICY", "d": "Fiscal policy is the government's use of taxation and government spending to influence the economy.", "e": "During COIVD 19, the US government used piscal policy by increasing government spending and providing financial support to households and businesses to support the economy.", "n": "Fiscal policy can foster economic growth and reduce unemployment, but it can also increase government debt and inflation.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "MONETARY POLICY", "d": "Monetary policy is the central bank's use of interest rates and money supply to influence the economy", "e": "During COVID 19, the US Federall Reserve used monetary policy by cutting interest rates to encourage borrowing and spending", "n": "Monetary can control inflation and foster economic growth, but it can take a long time to affect the economic, and it can also increase unemployment.", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "MONEY SUPPLY", "d": "Money supply is the total amount of money available in the economy at a particular time.", "e": "During COVID 19, the US money supply increased rapidly as the US Federal Seserve introduced measures to support the economy.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "ENDOGENOUS- EXOGENOUS", "d": "Endogenous is something that originates within a system, while exogenous is something that originates froom outside a system.", "e": "Endogenous factors: Apple improves its Iphone design to attract more customers.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "COST ACCOUNTING", "d": "Cost accounting is the process of caculating or analyzing the cost of producing products or providing a services", "e": "For example, a bakery uses cost accounting to calculate the cost of each cake, including the cost of ingredients, workers' wages and electricity. This process helps the bakery set a suitable selling price for each cake to earn a profit", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "TAX ACCOUNTING", "d": "Tax accounting is a process of calculating taxes and preparing tax reports, making sure that the company follows the tax laws.", "e": "A bakery uses tax accounting to calculate the amount of tax it has to pay to the tax authority based on its sales records and other expenses. This process ensures that the company complies with tax laws and pays taxes on time", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "MANAGERIAL MANAGEMENT ACCOUNTING", "d": "Management accounting is the process of providing financial information to help managers make business decisions.", "e": "A bakery uses management accounting to compare the costs and revenues of different types of cakes in order to decide which cakes to promote more. This process helps the company control costs and improve profitability.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "CREATIVE ACCOUNTING", "d": "Creative accounting is the use of accounting methods to present a company's financial result in a more favourable way, it sometime misleads investors", "e": "A bakery uses creative accounting to make its financial reports look better than they really are, such as oversating revenue or reporting higher net profits to attract more investors. These practises sometimes may violate the accounting rules.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "FINANCIAL ACCOUNTING", "d": "Financial accounting is the process of recording, summarising and reporting a company's financial transactions to provide information for external users.", "e": "A bakery uses financial accounting to record its revenue, total assets, liabilities to preparea financial statements to show the company's financial position and performance. for external users such as banks and investors.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "FORENSIC ACCOUNTING", "d": "Forensic accounting is the process of using accounting skills to investigate financial crimes.", "e": "A bakery suspects that one of is workers has stolen money from the company, so it can use forensic accounting to investigate and analyze transactions to find evidence of fraud.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "AUDITTING", "d": "Auditing is the process of examining a company's financial records and statements to check whether they are correct and comply with accounting rules.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "BOOKKEEPING", "d": "Bookkeeping is a process of recording company's daily transactions such as sales, purchases and payments.", "e": "A bakery uses bookkeeping to record its daily transactions and enter its into an accounting system. This process helps the company keep financial records and prepare fianancial statemtns.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "FINANCIAL STATEMENT", "d": "Financial statement is a report that can show a company's financial position and business performance. Financial statement usually includes balance sheet, cashflow statement and income statement.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "CASH FLOW STATEMENT", "d": "Cashflow statement is a financial statement that shows the cash coming into and going out of a business during a specific period.", "e": "A bakery uses cashflow statement to record the money received form customers and the money spend to purchase ingredients, workers' wage and electricity. This statement helps the owners understand the company's cash position and manage its money effectively", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "INCOME STATEMENT", "d": "Income statement is a financial statement that shows a company's revenue, expenses, profits and losses over a period of time.", "e": "A bakery uses an income statement to show its revenue and expenses. Based on this information, the accountant can calculate whether the company make a profit or loss", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "BALANCE SHEET", "d": "Balance sheet is a financial statement that shows asset, liability and equity of a company in a specific point time.", "e": "A bakery uses balance sheet to shows asset, liability and equity of its company. This information can show what the company owns, what the company owes and the company's owners. It can also help the owners understand the bakery's position.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "FINANICIAL TRANSACTIONS", "d": "Financial transaction is an activity that involves the exchange of money", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "CREDITORS", "d": "Creditor are people or businesses that the company owes money to", "e": "Apple has account payable to its suppliers. It means that Apple's suppliers are creditors as Apple owes them money for goods and services.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "DEPOSITORS", "d": "Depositors are people or businesses that put money into a bank account", "e": "A customer deposits 100 milion VND into a saving account in Vietcombank. So this customer is a depositor.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "LIQUIDITY", "d": "Liquidity is the company's ability to pays its short-term debts using its cash and curent assets that can be quickly converted into cash.", "e": "Amazon needs cash to pay its suppliers, if the company has enough cash and current assets to pay its short-term debts, so the company has good liquidity", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "SOLVENCY", "d": "Solvency is a company's ability to pays its long-term debts and meets its financial obligations.", "e": "Coca-Cola may borrow money to build a new factory. If the company has enough financial resources to repay the loan over time, so Coca-Cola has good solvency", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "REVENUE", "d": "Revenue is the money a company earns from selling goods and services before minusing expenses.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "COST OF GOOD SOLD (COGS)", "d": "Cost of good sold is the direct cost of producing the goods and services that a company sells during a specific period", "e": "Coca cola reported a large number in cost of good sold, includes the costs of raw materials, packaging and transportation activities.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "GROSS PROFIT - NET PROFIT/INCOME", "d": "Gross profit is revenue minus cost of good sold. Net profit is the amount of money left after minusing all expenses from revenue.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "OPERATING EXPENSES", "d": "Operating expenses arre the costs a company has to pay to run its business operations, excluding the cost of good sold.", "e": "Coca cola may pay for operating expenses such as rent, advertising costs, managers' salary", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "PAYABLE &RECEIVABLE", "d": "Account payable records the money that a company owes to its suppliers. Account receivable records the money that customers owe the company", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "INTANGIBLE & TANGIBLE ASSETS", "d": "Tangible assets are assets that have a specific physical form. While Intangible assets are assets that do not have a physical form but have the value to the company", "e": "Coca cola's tangible assets are its factories, buidings and equipment. While its intangible assets are trademarks and patents.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "ACCRUED EXPENSE & DEFERRED EXPENSE", "d": "Accured expenses are expenses that have been incurred but have not been paid yet. Deffered expenses are expenses paid in advance but have not yet fully recognized as expenses.", "e": "Coca cola records unpaid advertising costs as accured expense as the costs have not been paid yet. While, coca cola pays rent in advance for a warehouse for 10 months. The company records the payment as a deferred expense.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "GOODWILL", "d": "Goodwill is an intangible asset that arise when a company buys another comany for more than the value of is identifiable net assets.", "e": "In 2025, e.l.f beauty acquired rhode for approcimately 900 milion dollar and recorded about 500 milition dollar as goodwill, This goodwill reflected future growth of the brand", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "AMORTIZATION", "d": "Amortization is the process of spreading the cost of its intangible asset over its useful life.", "e": "A company spends 100 000 USD for buying sofware and uses it for 5 years. It records 20000 USD as an expense each year", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "DEPRECIATION", "d": "Depreciation is the process of spreading the cost of its tangible asset over its useful life", "e": "A company spends 100000 USD for buying computers and uses it for 5 year, so it records 20000 as an expanses each yaer", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "DIVIDENDS", "d": "A dividen is money a company pays to its shareholders.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "DEPOSIT", "d": "Deposit is the act of putting money into a bank account", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "LIQUIDITY-LIQUIDATED", "d": "Liquidity is how rapidly a business can turn its assets into cash or how business can get cash to pays its bills", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "COLLATERAL", "d": "Collateral is an asset that a borrower promises a lender as security for a loan", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "MORTAGE", "d": "Mortage is a loan used to buy property such as house or department", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "OVERDRAFT", "d": "Overdraft allows customer to spend more money than the available balance in a bank account, up to an agreed limit", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "CURRENT ACCOUNT", "d": "Current account is a bank account used for frequent deposits and payments", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "SAVING ACCOUNT", "d": "Saving account is a bank account used for saving purpose, it is designed to help people save money and earn interest", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "DEPOSIT ACCOUNT", "d": "Deposit account is a bank account in which customers keep their money", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "MATURITY DATE", "d": "A maturity date is the date when a loan or financial instument becomes due for repayment", "e": "If you by a bond that has a maturity period of 5 years from a company. After 5 years, the company must repay the 1 000 principle", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "A CREDIT CARDS", "d": "A credit card allows customer to borrow money from a card to pay for goods and services, up to a credit limit", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "A DEBIT CARD", "d": "A debit card allows customer to pay for goods and services using the money from their bank account", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "A LOAN - A DEBT", "d": "A loan is a money borrowed from a bank or another lender and must be repaid.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "FINANCIAL INTERMEDIARY", "d": "Financial intermediary is an organization that helps money moves from people who have money to people who need money", "e": "Vietcombank accepts deposits from customers and lends money to businesses and individuals", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "COMMERCIAL BANKS", "d": "Commercial bank is a banks that keeps people's money, gives loans and helps people make payments", "e": "Vietcombank is a commercial bank, it allows people to send money, tranfer money and and borrow money", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "INSURANCE COMPANY", "d": "An insurance company is a company that collects money from customers and pays them when the covered problems happen", "e": "Prudential Vietnam is an insurance company as it sells insurance policies to customers.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "PENSION FUND", "d": "pension fund is a fund that collects and invests money to pay people when they retire", "e": "the US Social Security system uses its trust funds to help pay retirement benefits to eligible retired workers", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "BUIDING SOCIETY", "d": "Building society is a financial organization that mainly accepts savings from members and provides loans to help people buy homes", "e": "Nationwilde is a building society as it accepts savings from customers and provides mortgages to people who want to buy houses.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "MONEY STOCK", "d": "Money stock is the total amount of money available in an economy at a particular time", "e": "If people and businesses hold their money in their bank accounts, these can be included in to the country's total money stock", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "TRUSTEE SAVING BANK", "d": "Trustee saving bank is a type of financial intermediary that was originally set up to help people save money safely. Today, banks offer many more financial services such as lending money and providing mortgages to people", "e": "Trustee Savings Bank (TSV) in the United Kingdom was created to help ordinary people save the money safely", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "CLEARING BANK", "d": "Clearing bank is a bank that helps tranfer money between different banks and makes sure that payments are settled correctly", "e": "Imagine you use Vietcombank to transfer money to someone uses BIDV. The banks need to process they payment between them. Clearing arrangements help the money move between the banks correctly.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "CLEARING SYSTEM", "d": "Clearing system helps banks calculate the money they owe each other and settle payment", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "HEDGE FUNDS", "d": "Hedge fund is a fund that collects money from investors and tries to invest these money to make a profit.", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "INVESTMENT BANKS", "d": "Investment bank is a financial institution that helps governments and companies raise money and provides advice on big financial deals. It is also a financial intermediary as it connects companies that need money with investors who have money to invest", "e": "Goldman Sachs is a famous investment bank in the United States. If a company wants to raise money to expand its business. Goldman Sachs can help company sells shares or bonds to investors", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "ISLAMIC BANKS", "d": "Islamic bank is a bank that follows Islamic law. It can not charge or pay traditional interest on loans and deposits as interest is prohibited under Islamic law. Therefore, the bank has to find other ways to make a profit without violating Islamic law", "e": "So if customers want to buy a house but they do not have enough money to buy it. They can not use a conventional - interest based loan under Islamic banking principles. The bank may buy the house and sells it to customer at an agreed higher price, which the customer pays over time.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "NON-BANK FINANCIAL INTERMEDIARIES (NBFIS)", "d": "Non-bank financial intermediary is a financial instituation that provides financial services but not a traditional bank.", "e": "Prudential is a non-bank financial intermediary and also a insurance company. Customers buy insurance from prudential and the company collecst premiums and invests fudns to help meet its future obligations to policyholders", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "PRIVATE BANKS", "d": "Private bank is a bank owned by private individuals or companies rather than the goverment", "e": "HDbank is a privately owned commercial bank", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "STOCKBROKERS", "d": "Stockbroker is a person or a company that buys and sells stocks on behalf of clients. Stockbroker acts as a financial intermediary. They connect investors who want to buy or sell securities with the stock market", "e": "If you want to buy shares of vinamilk but you can not trade directly on the stock exchange. so you use a securities company such as SSI Securities to help you place your buy order", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "A SHARE", "d": "A share represents ownership in a company", "e": "If you buy shares in Vienamilk worth 1 million VNd, you become a shareholder, which means that you own a small part of the company", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "BONDS", "d": "Bond is a way for a company or goverment to borrow money from investors. Buying bonds helps you become a bondholder, not a shareholder or an owner of the company", "e": "You buy a Vinamilk bond worth 1 million VND. It means that Vinamilk owes you 1 million VND and agrees to pay interest en return your money according to the bond's terms.", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "DEFAULT", "d": "Default means failing to make a required debt payment according to the argeed terms", "e": "If Vinamil fails topay the required interest of bonds according to the bond's terms, it may be in default", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "CREDIT CRUNCH", "d": "Credit crunch happens when a banks become less willing or able to lend money, making its difficult for people and businesses to borrow", "e": "A Credit crunch can happen when a banks are afraid that borrowers will not repay their loans, when banks have less money available to lend or during an economic ciris", "n": "", "s": "workbook"}, {"u": 11, "t": "PRODUCT PLACEMENT", "d": "Showing a brand in films or TV programmes as part of the story", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "OUT-OF-HOME (OOH)", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "CELEBRITY ENDORSEMENT", "d": "A famous person promotes a product", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "LEAFLET", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "SLOGAN", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "PRODUCT POSITIONING", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "POINT-OF-SALE ADVERTISING", "d": "Ads in the shop where the purchase happens", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "WORD-OF-MOUTH ADVERTISING", "d": "Recommendations passed between people", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "SPONSORSHIP", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "TV COMMERCIAL", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "PERR-TO-PEER NETWORKS", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "VIRAL MARKETING", "d": "Messages spread rapidly online from person to person", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "PUBLIC -RELATIONS CAMPAIGN", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "BRAND-SWITCHER", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "BRAND IMAGE", "d": "The public’s perception of a brand", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "BRAND LOYALTY", "d": "Customers’ tendency to keep buying the same brand", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "FREE SAMPLE", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "INDUSTRIAL BUYER", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "INITIAL TRIAL", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "LOSS LEADER", "d": "Product sold at a loss to attract customers", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 11, "t": "PRICE-CONSCIOUS", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "PURCHASING CYCLE", "d": "(definition not yet provided in the workbook)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook – needs definition"}, {"u": 11, "t": "REDEEMABLE COUPON", "d": "Voucher exchanged for a discount", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "workbook (term) + textbook/Revision (definition)"}, {"u": 1, "t": "leader", "d": "Someone with followers who are inspired voluntarily, not by formal authority", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "chain of command", "d": "The line of authority from top to bottom of an organization", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "matrix management", "d": "Staff report to both a functional manager and a project/product manager", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "flattening hierarchies", "d": "Removing management layers so decisions are made lower down", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "line manager", "d": "The boss to whom a person directly reports", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "decentralization", "d": "Passing decision-making power to lower levels or branches", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 3, "t": "delegation", "d": "Giving someone else responsibility and authority for a task", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "power distance", "d": "The extent to which less powerful people accept unequal distribution of power", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 4, "t": "deference", "d": "Respect shown to someone of higher status", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "internal recruitment", "d": "Filling a vacancy with someone already working in the company", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "external recruitment", "d": "Filling a vacancy with someone from outside the company", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "off-the-job training", "d": "Training away from the workplace, e.g. courses", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "job description", "d": "Document listing the duties and responsibilities of a job", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "aptitude test", "d": "Test measuring a candidate’s natural ability for a type of work", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 5, "t": "in-tray exercise", "d": "Selection task: deal with a pile of typical work documents under time pressure", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "primary sector", "d": "Extracts natural resources: farming, mining, fishing", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "secondary sector", "d": "Manufactures and processes goods", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "tertiary sector", "d": "Provides services", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "quaternary sector", "d": "Knowledge-based activities: IT, research, consulting", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "sole trader", "d": "Business owned and run by one person with unlimited liability", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "partnership", "d": "Business owned by two or more people who share profits and liability", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "franchise", "d": "Right to use a company’s brand and system in return for fees", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "joint venture", "d": "Two companies create a new business together and share risks and profits", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "privatization", "d": "Selling state-owned companies to private owners", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "limited liability", "d": "Owners lose only the money they invested if the company fails", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "stakeholder", "d": "Anyone affected by a company: employees, customers, suppliers, community", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 6, "t": "delocalize", "d": "Move production to another place, often abroad where costs are lower", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "just-in-time (JIT)", "d": "Receiving materials only when needed, so stock is kept minimal", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "economies of scale", "d": "Falling average cost as output increases", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "supply chain", "d": "The network of organizations that move a product from raw materials to customer", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "planned obsolescence", "d": "Designing products to become out of date or fail after a limited time", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "zero defects", "d": "Quality goal of making no faulty products at all", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "optimum capacity", "d": "The level of output at which average cost is lowest", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "excess capacity", "d": "Ability to produce more than the market demands; leads to idle plant and higher unit costs", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 7, "t": "product recall", "d": "Manufacturer asks customers to return a faulty product", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "logistics", "d": "Managing the flow of goods from procurement to customer service", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "push strategy", "d": "Producing in advance and pushing products to market based on forecasts", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "pull strategy", "d": "Producing in response to actual customer demand", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "inbound logistics", "d": "Moving materials from suppliers into the company", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "outbound logistics", "d": "Moving finished goods from the company to customers", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 8, "t": "kanban", "d": "Signal-card system that triggers production or replenishment as stock is used", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "quality control", "d": "Inspecting products to find defects", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "quality assurance", "d": "Preventing defects by managing the whole process", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "Total Quality Management (TQM)", "d": "Everyone in the organization is responsible for quality", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "benchmarking", "d": "Comparing your performance with the best in the industry", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "durability", "d": "How long a product lasts", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "reliability", "d": "How consistently a product works without failure", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "serviceability", "d": "How easily a product can be repaired", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "warranty", "d": "Written promise to repair or replace a faulty product", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "tolerance", "d": "Permitted amount of variation from a specification", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "six sigma", "d": "Method aiming for almost no defects (3.4 per million)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "key performance indicator (KPI)", "d": "Measurable value showing how well objectives are being achieved", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "lagging indicator", "d": "Measures past results (e.g. defects found, profit)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 9, "t": "leading indicator", "d": "Predicts future performance (e.g. staff training hours)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market leader", "d": "Company with the largest market share", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market challenger", "d": "Company trying to take share from the leader", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market niche", "d": "Small specialized part of a market", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market segmentation", "d": "Dividing a market into groups with similar needs", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market share", "d": "A company’s sales as a percentage of total market sales", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "marketing mix", "d": "The 4 Ps: product, price, place, promotion", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market skimming", "d": "Setting a high price for a new product to maximize revenue before competitors appear", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "market penetration", "d": "Setting a low price to win a large share quickly", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "product life cycle", "d": "Stages: introduction, growth, maturity, decline", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "price elasticity", "d": "How much demand changes when price changes", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "product differentiation", "d": "Making a product different from competitors’", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "early adopters", "d": "First customers to buy a new product", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 10, "t": "distribution channel", "d": "Route a product takes from producer to consumer (e.g. wholesaler, retailer)", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "liquidity", "d": "How quickly an asset can be turned into cash", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "mortgage", "d": "Loan to buy property, secured on that property", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 12, "t": "bond", "d": "Debt security: investor lends money and gets interest, no ownership", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "management accounting", "d": "Information prepared for managers’ internal decisions", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "COGS", "d": "Cost of goods sold: direct cost of producing what was sold", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "gross profit", "d": "Revenue minus cost of goods sold", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 13, "t": "net profit", "d": "Profit after all expenses and tax", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "depression", "d": "A deep and prolonged recession", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "Keynesianism", "d": "Theory that government should boost demand in a recession", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 14, "t": "potential output", "d": "Output at full employment", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "ethical dilemma", "d": "Choice between two actions that may both be morally wrong", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "business ethics", "d": "Standards of business behaviour that promote human welfare", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 15, "t": "ethical lapse", "d": "Temporary failure to act in the correct way", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "rationalization", "d": "Making a company more effective, often by cutting activities or jobs", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "redundancy package", "d": "Payments and benefits for workers who lose their jobs", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 16, "t": "job insecurity", "d": "Fear of losing your job", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "tariff", "d": "Tax on imported goods", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "infant industry", "d": "New industry that cannot yet compete with established foreign firms", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}, {"u": 17, "t": "strategic industry", "d": "Industry essential to a locality’s growth or stability", "e": "", "n": "", "s": "Revision.docx / textbook"}], "essays": [{"u": 1, "k": "material", "x": "The main difference is that managers have subordinates who obey them because of their job title and formal authority, whereas leades have followers who follow them voluntarily out of trust and inspiration. Managers focus on maintaining order and system while leaders focus on driving change and vision"}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "It changes what task the employee do, but does not increase task complexity or decision-making power"}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "job rotation means regularly moving between different tasks in order to reduces the boredom and helps employee learns new skills"}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "for example, a sales employees can move to the marketing department and work here for 3 months before returning to their original department."}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "It increase task quantity to make work less repetitive, but do not increase authority"}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "job enlargement means adding the same level tasks for employee to make a job more varied. for example, a sale offcial at first just need to find new customers, but now the managers ask her to answer the customers'  feedbacks and make a sale report. job enlargement makes a daily job become more interesting by adding new tasks at the same level. But it can also increase the workload for the employee, which can be stressful and overwhelmed."}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "It expands the job by giving the employees greater responisibility and decision-marking power"}, {"u": 2, "k": "material", "x": "job enrichment means adding the higher-level tasks for employees in order to gives them responsibiloity and the ability to making decisions. for example, a sales worker at first just do task that the manager gives, know she can deal the discount direactly with the customer without asking for the manager's permission. Job enlargement can increase the motivation among workers, as it provide more reponsibility and increase the ability to get a promotion at work."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture helps people build trust and maintain good relationships because they listen carefully and avoid direct conflict. However, decisions may take more time, and problems may not be discussed openly."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture makes work organized and helps employees follow clear plans and deadlines. However, it can be less flexible when unexpected problems happen or when employees need to change plans quickly."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture can make communication more friendly and help people build strong personal relationships. However, doing many things at the same time can make work less organized and may cause some tasks to be forgotten."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "Glocalization helps companies attract local customers because products fit local tastes and cultures better. However, changing products for different countries can increase costs and make the business more difficult to manage."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture makes rules and decisions more consistent because everyone follows the same standards. However, it can be difficult to deal with special situations when following the same rule is not the best solution."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture encourages teamwork, loyalty, and support between employees. However, employees may have less freedom to make individual decisions or focus on their own goals."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture allows people to consider personal relationships and special situations when making decisions. However, decisions may sometimes seem unfair because different people may receive different treatment."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture encourages independence, personal responsibility, and individual achievement. However, employees may focus too much on their own goals and may cooperate less with other team members."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "Understanding power distance helps managers choose the right way to communicate with employees from different cultures. However, a misunderstanding of power distance can create communication problems between managers and employees."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture helps people communicate politely and protect relationships, especially when discussing sensitive topics. However, messages may be misunderstood because people do not always say exactly what they mean."}, {"u": 4, "k": "material", "x": "This culture makes communication clear and reduces the need to guess the meaning of a message. However, very direct communication may sometimes sound too strong or unfriendly in cultures that prefer indirect communication."}, {"u": 5, "k": "material", "x": "Aptitude tests are usually formal tests that applicants can complete in a short time, while an in-tray exercise usually requires more time. Aptitude tests mainly assess applicants in a theoretical way, while an in-tray exercise measures their ability to solve real-life problems and deal with situations that might happen at work."}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "The primary sector is a part of the economy that extract the natural resources from the Earth and produces raw materials"}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "Vinacom is the example of the company in the primary sector because they extract natural gas  and coal from the earth to provide raw materials for other industries."}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "The primary sector provides the raw material for factories and businesses. It can bring a lot of money to a country, but it depends heavily on the weather and the availability of natural resources."}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "the secondary sector is a part of the economy that uses raw materials from the primary sector ro manufacture products."}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "Vinfast and Foxcon are the examples of the secondary sector because they use the raw materials such as metals, batteries and electronic components to manufacture finished products such as vehicles and smartphones"}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "The secondary sector creates many jobs in factories. However, companies need a lot of money to buy machines and build factories."}, {"u": 6, "k": "material", "x": "A joint venture is usually formed between two or more companies for a specific project, while a partnership is usually owned and run by two or more individuals who share the profits, responsibilities, and debts."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Lean production vs. Kaizen: Lean production is an overall way of managing production to reduce waste and improve efficiency. Kaizen is a specific practice of making small and continuous improvements to work."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "lean production is a way of producing products while using as little waste, time and effort as possible."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Toyota uses lean production throught its Toyota Production System (TPS). It uses Just-in-time to receive parts when they are needed, reducing excess inventory."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Lean production reduces waste and lowers storing cost of extra materials."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Kaizen vs. Innovation: Kaizen focuses on making small improvements regularly, often based on employees’ ideas. Innovation usually involves creating something new or making a major change, often through new technology or new ideas."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Kaizen is a Japanese idea of making small and regular improvements to work and reducing waste"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Honda encourages workers to suggest small changes every day, such as changing the position of tools to reduce unneccessary movement and reduce mistakes."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Kaizen do not require a lot of money because improvements come form employees' ideas and small changes. However, it takes time and support from the management."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Cell production vs. Flow production: Cell production divides workers and machines into small teams, with each team making a specific part or module of a product. Flow production uses a continuous production line where workers usually perform one specific task repeatedly."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Cell production is a production method where workers and machines are divided into different groups called cells, and each cell makes a specific part of the finished product."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Toyota uses a cell production by grouping workers and machines into small production cells. One cells can be responsible for assembling the car seats, while another cell ressembles the dashboard. Worker in each cells work together to complete their specific part before sending it to the next stage of production"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Cell production can increase the motivation of workers because they might feel more responsible when they work in small group. However, the total production may lower than with a continuous flow production line."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Job production vs. Batch production: Job production makes one product at a time, usually according to one customer's specific requirements. Batch production makes a group of the same products together before moving to another product."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Job production is a production method where a product is made individually and specially for one customer's order"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Rolls-Royce uses job production to manufacture luxury cars according to rach customer's requirements. For example, a customer can choose the car's color, materials and special features. Each car is made to meet the customer's specific preferences."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Job production allows companies to make high-quality products that are exactly what the customer wants. However, making products individually takes more time and makes each product much more expensive to produce"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Batch production vs. Flow production: Batch production makes products in groups and may stop between batches to change or reset the machines. Flow production makes large quantities of products continuously with very little stopping."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "batch production is a production method where a large number of the same products are made together before moving to another product."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Toyota uses a batch production by manufactoring a group of cars of the same model before switching to another model. For example, it may produce a batch of Toyota Corolla cars before moving to Toyota Camry cars"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Batch production allows company to produce difference tyoes of products using the same machines. However, the company may need to store a large quantity of finished products and the production time can be lost when the machines changed to another new batch"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Flow production vs. Job production: Flow production makes large quantities of standardized products continuously, so the cost per product is usually low. Job production makes individual products for specific customers, so the cost per product is usually higher."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Flow production is a production method where large quantities of the same produce are made continuously on the production line."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Coca cola and Foxconn use flow production  by producing continuously large quantities of goods such as cans of soft drinks and electronic devices on the production line."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Flow production can create a large number of goods quickly at a low cost per product. But setting up the machines is very expensive and the production line is not flexible when the company wants to change the product design"}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Lead time vs. Delivery time: Lead time is the total time from when an order is placed until the order is completed and received. Delivery time usually refers to the time it takes for the product to travel from the seller or warehouse to the customer."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "lead time is the total time it takes to deliver goods from a very first stage of productions to customers. lead time included the producing time of the goods, the time it takes to transport raw materials from the suppliers to the company, while delivery time is just the time it takes to deliver the finished goods from comany's warehouse to customers."}, {"u": 7, "k": "material", "x": "Delivery time vs. Lead time: Delivery time focuses mainly on how long it takes to transport a product to the customer. Lead time covers the whole process, from receiving the order to completing and delivering it."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "Push vs Pull: Push produces goods based on forecasts, while Pull produces goods based on actual customer demand."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "**Push strategy** is a strategy of producing a large quantity of goods in advance based on sales forecasts.\n\nThis strategy ensures the availability of goods when customers want to buy them in large quantities. However, it also increases the risk for the company when forecasts are **inaccurate**, which can result in **excess inventory**.\n\nFor example, Coca-Cola often uses a push strategy because it produces cans of soft drinks based on sales forecasts for different seasons.**"}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "in constrast, pull strategy is a strategy of producing goods after a customer places an order. this strategy ensure the correct number of goods that are needed to server for customer's demand, prevent  the ability of excess inventory. But when there is a sudden demand, the company will not have enough inventory to server for customers. for example, DELL uses a pull strategy because it only produce laptops and electronic devices after receiving an order from customer."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "Inbound vs Outbound logistics: Inbound logistics moves raw materials and supplies into a company, while outbound logistics moves finished products out to customers."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "inbound logistics is a process of transporting raw materials and supplies to a company. for example, cocacola inbound its materials such as water, sugar and packagings for softdrinks productions"}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "Outbound vs Inbound logistics: Outbound logistics focuses on delivering finished products to customers, while inbound logistics focuses on bringing materials from suppliers to the company."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "outbound losgistics is a process of transporting finished products from a comany to customers. for example, coca cola uses outbound losigstics to transport cans of softdrinks to their customers."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "Customs clearance vs Freight forwarding: Customs clearance deals with documents, taxes, and legal requirements for crossing borders, while freight forwarding arranges the transportation of goods."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "customs clearane is the process of asking for permission of the custom authority to export and inport goods after paying all the tax fees and extra fees. Many global coporates when exporting their products to the wordwide market need to prepare the information and product documents clearly in order to be accepted by the local and international customs clearances. The customs clearence will ensure that there is no forbidden goods can inport or export out of the country. It is the checking step for evey goods that are gone in and out."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "Reverse logistics vs Forward logistics: Reverse logistics moves products from customers back to the company, while forward logistics moves products from the company to customers."}, {"u": 8, "k": "material", "x": "reverse logistics is the process of moving goods from the customer back to the business because of trade-in, returns or recycling.  Apple uses a reverse logistics to collect the valuable materials such as copper, gold, aluminium from their old products such as ipad, laptop and iphone to recycle it for new production purposes. this reversal logistic allows company to collect and reuse the materials form their products."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Despite the benefits of free trade, governments often impose barriers to protect domestic industries or achieve other policy objectives."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Analyze the impact of globalization on developed and developing countries."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Body 1 — Advantages\n- Lower prices: fewer tariffs make imported products cheaper.\n- More choices: consumers can buy products from different countries.\n- Economic growth: businesses can access international markets and increase sales.\n- More competition: companies improve product quality to compete with foreign businesses.\nBody 2 — Disadvantages\n- Local businesses struggle: small businesses may find it difficult to compete with large foreign companies.\n- Job losses: local companies may close or reduce workers.\n- Dependence on other countries: countries may rely too much on imported essential goods.\nConclusion: Free trade benefits consumers and businesses, but governments should support local industries affected by foreign competition."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss different types of trade barriers, such as tariffs, quotas, and subsidies."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Analyze the arguments for and against protectionist trade policies and use examples from your Business Studies Course to illustrate these points."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Explain the advantages and disadvantages of free trade."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Body 1 — Developed countries\n- Businesses can enter new markets and sell more products.\n- Companies may move production abroad to reduce costs.\n- However, some domestic workers may lose jobs when production moves overseas.\nBody 2 — Developing countries\n- Foreign investment can create jobs and bring new technology.\n- Local businesses may gain access to international markets.\n- However, small local businesses may struggle to compete with large multinational corporations.\n- Workers may face low wages or poor working conditions in some industries."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Free trade, Protectionism"}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Infant Industry, Stategic industry"}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss the benefits and challenges of globalization for businesses and economies."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Introduction: Dùng mở bài 2.\n\nBody 1 — Benefits\n- Access to new markets and more consumers → more sales.\n- Outsourcing and offshoring → lower costs or access to specialized workers.\n- Cultural exchange → people share ideas, products and ways of working.\nBody 2 — Challenges\n- Increased competition → local businesses may struggle.\n- Moving production abroad → some workers in the original country may lose jobs.\n- Differences in language and culture may create communication problems.\nConclusion: Dùng kết bài có đề xuất."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss the arguments for and against protectionist trade policies."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Introduction: Dùng mở bài 1.\n\nBody 1 — Arguments for protectionism\n- Protect local jobs and businesses from foreign competition.\n- Give infant industries time to grow.\n- Support strategic industries that are important to the country.\n- Governments may use tariffs, quotas or subsidies.\nBody 2 — Arguments against protectionism\n- Imported goods may become more expensive.\n- Consumers may have fewer choices.\n- Local businesses may face less pressure to improve.\n- Other countries may introduce their own trade barriers in response.\nConclusion: Dùng kết bài có đề xuất: governments should balance protection with the benefits of trade."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Globalization has led to increased interconnectedness and interdependence among nations."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss the benefits and challenges of globalization for businesses and economies, considering:"}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Access to new markets and consumersn, Outsourcing and offshoring,   Cultural exchange and diversity"}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Explain how international trade affects a country's economy."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Introduction: Dùng mở bài 1.\n\nBody 1 — Benefits of international trade\n- Countries can sell products to foreign markets.\n- They can specialize in products they produce relatively efficiently.\n- Trade can create jobs and give consumers more choices.\nBody 2 — Possible challenges\n- Local businesses may face strong foreign competition.\n- Imports may be greater than exports, creating a trade deficit in goods.\n- A trade surplus occurs when exports of goods are greater than imports of goods.\n- A trade balance alone does not show the full health of an economy.\nConclusion: Dùng kết bài trung lập."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Outsourcing and offshoring"}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Cultural exchange and diversity"}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Analyze the impact of globalization on developed and developing countries, supported by examples from what you have learned in your Business Studies Course."}, {"u": 17, "k": "prompt", "x": "Explain why governments impose trade barriers."}, {"u": 17, "k": "material", "x": "Introduction\n- Governments impose trade barriers mainly to protect local industries and support the economy.\nBody 1 — Protect local businesses and jobs\n- Cheap foreign products make it difficult for local businesses to compete.\n- Tariffs and quotas reduce foreign competition and help protect local jobs.\nBody 2 — Support important industries\n- Infant industries: need time to grow and compete with foreign companies.\n- Strategic industries: such as food and defense, are important for national security.\n- Governments may use subsidies or tariffs to support these industries.\nBody 3 — Prevent unfair trade\n- Foreign companies may sell products at unfairly low prices (dumping).\n- Governments may impose anti-dumping duties to protect local producers.\nConclusion\n- Trade barriers protect businesses, jobs and important industries, but excessive restrictions may increase prices for consumers."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Improving workplace efficiency is essential for reducing costs, increasing productivity and enhancing competitiveness"}, {"u": 16, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss the reasons for redundancy and its effects on employees and businesses."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Introduction: There are several reasons why [situation] happens. It can affect both [group 1] and [group 2] in different ways.\n\nBody 1 — Reasons\n- Economic pressure: sales fall, so businesses need to reduce costs.\n- Technology: machines can replace some tasks done by workers.\n- Strategic changes: restructuring or moving production abroad means some jobs are no longer needed.\nBody 2 — Implications\n- For employees: loss of income, job insecurity and difficulty finding a new job.\n- For the company: lower costs and potentially higher efficiency, but possible loss of experienced workers and lower morale.\n- Solutions: redundancy packages, retraining and support to improve employability.\nConclusion: In conclusion, [topic] can bring important benefits, but its challenges should not be ignored. Businesses / Governments should take suitable steps to maximize the benefits and reduce the negative effects."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Delocalization, Restructuring"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Process improvement and automation"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Effective communication and collaboration"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Employee motivation and engagement"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Employee motivation and engagement"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "rationalization: improve production processes and reduce waste"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Provide examples of companies that have sucessfully implemented efficiency improvement initiatives, supported by examples from what you have learned in your Business Study Course"}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Restructuring, Contracting out, Delayering"}, {"u": 16, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss how businesses can improve efficiency and reduce costs."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "In today's business world, [topic] is an important issue. It can bring several benefits, but it may also create some challenges. This essay will discuss its main effects.                                                                                                                                                                                                          Body 1 — Process improvement and automation\n- Businesses can use machines to do repetitive tasks.\n- They can improve work processes and reduce waste.\n- Result: save time and money; increase productivity.\n- Vocabulary: automation, rationalization, process improvement.\nBody 2 — Organizational changes\n- Businesses can restructure departments or remove unnecessary management levels.\n- They can contract out some services to outside companies.\n- Result: lower costs and faster decisions.\n- Drawback: employees may feel stressed or lose their jobs.\n- Vocabulary: restructuring, contracting out, delayering.\nConclusion: In conclusion, [topic] can bring important benefits, but its challenges should not be ignored. Businesses / Governments should take suitable steps to maximize the benefits and reduce the negative effects."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Redundancy is often a result of various factors within an organization. Understanding these reasons is crucial for addressing the impacts of redundancy effectively"}, {"u": 16, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss the primary reasons for redundancy in modern businesses and their implications for employees and the organization."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Your discussion should include how factors such as economic pressures, technological changes and stategic shifts lead to redundacy as well as the consequences for affected employees and the organization's operational efficiency. Provide examples to illustrate these points."}, {"u": 16, "k": "prompt", "x": "Compare different forms of employment."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Body 1 — Full-time vs part-time\n- Full-time: stable income, employee benefits, but less free time.\n- Part-time: flexible schedule, more free time, but lower income and fewer benefits.\nBody 2 — Permanent vs temporary/contract work\n- Permanent: greater job security and long-term career opportunities.\n- Temporary/contract: useful for short-term projects, but less job security and uncertain income after the contract ends.\nConclusion\n\n- The best form of employment depends on a person's needs, financial situation and career goals"}, {"u": 16, "k": "prompt", "x": "Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of organizational changes."}, {"u": 16, "k": "material", "x": "Organizational changes help companies adapt to new business conditions, but they can also create challenges.\n\nBody 1 — Advantages\n- Improve efficiency: restructure departments and remove unnecessary steps to save time and costs.\n- Increase competitiveness: adopt new technology and respond to market changes.\n- Improve performance: employees may develop new skills and use better working methods.\nBody 2 — Disadvantages\n- Employee stress: workers may feel worried about new roles or possible job losses.\n- Resistance to change: employees may find new systems difficult to accept.\n- High costs: training employees and introducing new technology can be expensive.\nConclusion\n\n- Organizational changes can improve business performance, but companies should communicate clearly and support employees during the process."}, {"u": 15, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 1: In the modern business environment, companies are increasingly expected to engage in socially responsible practices. Analyze the long-term benefits and challenges of aligning corporate social responsibility with profit objectives."}, {"u": 15, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 2: Compare the stockholder model and the stakeholder model. Which approach is better for modern businesses?"}, {"u": 15, "k": "material", "x": "Idea 1: Improve company reputation\n- Customers prefer companies that care about society and the environment.\n- The company can build a good image and gain public trust.\n\nIdea 2: Build customer loyalty\n- Customers may continue buying from a responsible company.\n- This can increase sales and revenue over time.\n\nIdea 3: Support long-term growth\n- Reducing waste can lower operating costs.\n- Fair treatment can improve employee motivation and productivity.\n\nPARAGRAPH 2: CHALLENGES\nIdea 1: High costs\n- Environmentally friendly materials may cost more.\n- Training workers and improving working conditions also require money.\n- These costs can reduce short-term profits.\n\nIdea 2: Slow results\n- CSR may take years to bring benefits.\n- Managers may face pressure to meet short-term profit targets.\n\nIdea 3: Difficult decisions\n- Companies must balance social needs and business goals.\n- Spending too much without a clear plan can put pressure on the budget."}, {"u": 15, "k": "material", "x": "A. Stockholder model\n- Main idea: Focuses on maximizing profits and increasing shareholder value.\n- Who matters most? Shareholders — people who own shares in a company.\n- Main objective: Help shareholders earn money from their investment.\n- Example: Apple buys back its own shares and returns money to shareholders.\n- Explanation: These actions can increase the value shareholders receive from the company.\nB. Stakeholder model\n- Main idea: Considers the interests of all groups affected by business activities.\n- Who matters? Employees, customers, suppliers, shareholders and the environment.\n- Main objective: Balance different interests and create long-term value.\n- Example: Unilever promotes responsible practices among its suppliers and works to reduce environmental impacts.\n- Comparison: Unlike the stockholder model, the stakeholder model considers more than shareholders and profits.\nIII. Body Paragraph 2: Why is the stakeholder model better? (70–85 words)\nA. Benefit 1: Build customer trust\n- Treat customers fairly and provide responsible products.\n- Customers develop trust in the company.\n- They may continue buying its products.\n- Result: Customer loyalty and potential long-term revenue growth.\nB. Benefit 2: Improve employee performance\n- Treat employees fairly and support their needs.\n- Employees feel more motivated.\n- They may work more effectively.\n- Result: Higher productivity and better business performance.\nC. Benefit 3: Protect the environment\n- Reduce waste and environmental damage.\n- Show responsibility towards society.\n- Build a positive company reputation.\n- Result: Better long-term relationships with customers and the community."}, {"u": 15, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 3: \"When corporations focus solely on short-term profits without regulatory or moral constraints, they may engage in unethical or undemocratic actions that harm workers, local communities, and society.*\n\nExamine how unethical corporate practices damage a company's reputation and explain why corporate governance must protect human rights and community welfare.\""}, {"u": 15, "k": "material", "x": "TOPIC 4: \"While many multinational corporations claim to be committed to Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) and environmental sustainability, critics argue that many of these initiatives are merely 'greenwashing' aimed at protecting brand image.\n\nAnalyze the phenomenon of greenwashing in modern business. How can stakeholders distinguish between genuine corporate social responsibility and deceptive environmental marketing?\""}, {"u": 15, "k": "material", "x": "II. Body Paragraph 1: How do unethical practices damage a company's reputation? (65–80 words)\nA. Lose customer trust\n- Companies may treat workers unfairly or mislead customers.\n- Customers may lose trust in the company.\n- They may stop buying its products.\n- Result: Lower sales and a negative public image.\nB. Face legal and financial consequences\n- Unethical actions may violate laws or regulations.\n- Companies may face fines, lawsuits or compensation costs.\n- Investors may become less willing to invest.\n- Result: Financial losses and damage to the company's reputation.\nC. Real-life example: Volkswagen\n- Volkswagen used software to cheat emissions tests in certain diesel vehicles.\n- The company promoted these vehicles as environmentally friendly.\n- The scandal damaged public trust and led to legal and financial consequences.\n- Link to the topic: Dishonest business practices can harm both society and a company's reputation.\nIII. Body Paragraph 2: Why must corporate governance protect human rights and community welfare? (70–85 words)\nA. Protect workers' human rights\n- Establish clear rules for fair wages and safe working conditions.\n- Prevent exploitation, discrimination and unfair treatment.\n- Ensure that managers and suppliers follow these rules.\n- Result: Workers are treated fairly and can work in a safer environment.\nB. Protect local communities and society\n- Require companies to control pollution and reduce environmental damage.\n- Prevent business activities from harming people's health and living conditions.\n- Listen to community concerns before making important decisions.\n- Result: Better relationships between companies and local communities.\nC. Ensure accountability\n- Monitor business decisions and company activities.\n- Require managers to take responsibility for unethical actions.\n- Follow laws and ethical standards instead of focusing only on short-term profits.\n- Result: More responsible business practices and greater public trust."}, {"u": 15, "k": "material", "x": "II. Body Paragraph 1: How does greenwashing affect modern businesses? (65–80 words)\nA. Mislead customers\n- Companies make false, unclear or exaggerated environmental claims.\n- Customers believe that products are more environmentally friendly than they really are.\n- Customers may buy products based on misleading information.\n- Result: Customers make decisions without understanding the product's real environmental impact.\nB. Damage corporate reputation\n- The truth may be discovered by customers, journalists or environmental organizations.\n- Customers may lose trust in the company.\n- They may stop buying its products.\n- Result: Negative publicity, reduced customer loyalty and possible financial losses.\nC. Real-life example: H&M\n- H&M promoted its Conscious collection using environmental claims.\n- In 2022, Dutch authorities raised concerns that some claims were unclear or insufficiently supported.\n- H&M agreed to change or stop certain claims.\n- Link to the topic: Environmental marketing without clear evidence can create doubts about a company's CSR commitments.\nIII. Body Paragraph 2: How can stakeholders identify genuine CSR? (70–85 words)\nA. Check evidence and specific information\n- Look for clear, measurable environmental targets.\n- Check whether the company explains how its products reduce environmental harm.\n- Avoid relying only on words such as “green,” “eco-friendly” or “sustainable.”\n- Result: Stakeholders can evaluate whether claims are supported by facts.\nB. Look for independent verification\n- Check reports or certifications from credible independent organizations.\n- Compare company claims with reliable information from environmental groups or regulators.\n- Result: Independent evidence makes misleading claims easier to identify.\nC. Examine the company's overall practices\n- Check whether the company reduces waste, pollution and resource use in its operations and supply chain.\n- Compare its public promises with its actual performance.\n- Result: Genuine CSR requires real action and accountability, rather than advertising alone."}, {"u": 14, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 1: Discuss the advantage and disadvantage of government intervention in managing the business cycle"}, {"u": 14, "k": "material", "x": "Government can influence the economy by introducing fiscal policy. During an economic downturn, the government can increase its spending and adjust taxation to support the economy. For example, during the COVID-19 pandemic, the US government used fiscal policy by increasing government spending and providing financial support to households and businesses affected by the pandemic. By using this policy, the government can protect local businesses and industries during an economic crisis and reduce unemployment. Furthermore, it can also stimulate overall economic growth.\n\nHowever, fiscal policy can have some drawbacks. First of all, increasing government spending can cause inflation, which reduces the purchasing power of money in a country. Secondly, it can increase government debt if the government has to borrow money to stimulate the economy."}, {"u": 14, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 2: Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of using fiscal policy to influence the economic activity."}, {"u": 14, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 3: Discuss the advantage and disadvantages of using the monetary policy to maintain economic stability."}, {"u": 14, "k": "material", "x": "\"Monetary policy is the central bank's use of interest rates and the money supply to influence the economy. During an economic downturn, the central bank may cut interest rates to support the economy. For example, during the COVID-19 pandemic, the US Federal Reserve cut its key interest rate to nearly zero to encourage spending and investment. As a result, businesses could borrow money at lower interest rates and continue their operations during the economic crisis.\n\nHowever, monetary policy can take a long time to affect the economy. Furthermore, very low interest rates can contribute to inflation if demand grows too quickly, while very high interest rates can slow economic growth.\""}, {"u": 14, "k": "material", "x": "Fiscal policy is the government's use of taxation and government spending to influence the economy. For example, during the COVID-19 pandemic, the US government used fiscal policy by increasing government spending and providing financial support to households and businesses affected by the economic downturn. Fiscal policy can reduce unemployment and stimulate economic growth. By introducing such policies, the government can protect local businesses and industries during an economic crisis.\n\nHowever, increasing government spending can lead to inflation, which reduces the purchasing power of money. It can also increase government debt if the government has to borrow money to stimulate the economy."}, {"u": 14, "k": "material", "x": "Monetary policy is the central bank's use of interest rates and the money supply to influence the economy. During an economic downturn, the central bank may cut interest rates to support the economy. For example, during the COVID-19 pandemic, the US Federal Reserve cut its key interest rate to nearly zero to encourage spending and investment. As a result, businesses could borrow money at lower interest rates and continue their operations during the economic crisis.\n\nHowever, monetary policy can take a long time to affect the economy. Furthermore, very low interest rates can contribute to inflation if demand grows too quickly, while very high interest rates can slow economic growth."}, {"u": 13, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 1: Explain why accounting is importance to a business"}, {"u": 13, "k": "material", "x": "In today's business world, accounting plays a vital role in helping  companies manage their daily transactions. It is noticeable that the role of an accountant is now more and more essential for businesses. First of all, accounting is the language of business. Through various accounting activities, investors, banks and the tax authorities can understand the financial position of a company. Therefore, one of the main roles of an accountant is to record daily transactions of a company and turn this information into financial statements for internal and external users. Financial statements provide managers of the company with information about the business performance, help them develop future strategy for the company. Furthermore, financial statement can be used to attract more investment from shareholders and banks. It is clear that accounting can support the growth of the company. Accounting is really essential also because it will record all the daily transactions of the company, so through analyzing and summeraising steps, the owners will know whether the company makes a profit or a loss in order to decide to change or maintain the present strategies of the whole company. Moreover, tax accounting activity also calculates the amount of tax that a company has to pay for the tax authority, ensures that the company comply with tax laws. In  conclusion, Accountant is a person who reports all transactions of a company, prepares financial statements and ensure that the company complies with tax laws and accounting standards"}, {"u": 13, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 2: Explain the purpose of three main financial statements"}, {"u": 13, "k": "material", "x": "In today's business world, it is clear that financial statements play a vital role in providing investors and banks specific financial information about companies' performance. There are three main financial statements provide different information of the general financial statement, including balance sheet, income statement and cashflow statement. First of all, balance sheet is one of the three main statements of the financial statement that shows a snapshot of a company position during a specific point in time. Balance sheet includes three main catergories, assets, liabilities and equity, which shows what the company owns, what the company owes and the owners of the company respectively. Meanwhile, income statement shows whether the company makes a profit or a loss of a company. This statement analyzes the total revenue and total expenses of the company over a period of time to calculate the net profit and reports it. Last but not least, cash flow statement is the statement that illustrates the amount of cash inside the business. It will record where the cash comes from and where the cash goes. It helps the owners know the cash position of a company."}, {"u": 13, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 3: How can financial statements help people evaluate a company's financial performance"}, {"u": 13, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 4: Explain the different types of accounting and their purposes"}, {"u": 13, "k": "material", "x": "In the modern business environment, it is clear that accounting increasingly becomes a vital role in the business environment. It is also noticeable that there are different type of accountingin today's economy with different purposes. Normally, people are often familiar with financial and management accounting. These are some basic and essential accounting roles in a company. While financial accounting is a process of recording all daily transactions of a company and summarising this information in finanancial statements for external users such as investors, banks and tax authorities, management accounting is a process of analyzing all transactions to report to managers to help them make decitions and develop future strategies for the company. In some companies, the tax accountant is also really important, as they will calculate the amount of tax that the company has to pay to the tax authorities based on various data about the daily transactions, ensuring that the company complies with tax laws and accounting standards.Besides these three types of accounting , there are still other types such as forensic accounting and cost accounting. Forensic accounting is the activity that uses accounting skills to investigate financial crimes. For example, if a company suspects one of its workers has stolen money, then it can use forensic accounting to find evidence of fraud. On the order hand, cost accounting is the process of calculating the total expenses that the company has to pay in order to produce a product. In conclusion, it is clear that there are various accounting roles in the business environment, each type serves different purposes and contributes to effective management of a business."}, {"u": 13, "k": "prompt", "x": "TOPIC 5: why should companies prepare accurate and transparent financial statement"}, {"u": 13, "k": "material", "x": "In the modern business environment,  financial statements play a vital role in evaluating a company's financial position and business performance. The more favourable the figures appear, the more likely a company is to acttract investment. While presenting financial statement positively is really important, business must also prepare accurate and transparent financial statements for several important reasons. First and foremost, transparent financial statements protect a business reputation and company's credibility. Investors and banks not only seek companies with strong financial performance to invest in, but also value a trustworthy company that has accurate financial statementes giving them greater confidence in their investment decisions. If a company falsifies its financial statements to make the figures appear more favourable, it may lose its reputation and credibility. Furthermore, falsifying financial statements may violate accounting standards and laws, potentially resulting in legal consequences and misleading investors. If a company violates laws, it may face legal penalties by the local authorities and the government. In conclusion, companies need to prepare and present financial statements accurately in order to maintain their credibility and ensure compliance with the laws and accounting standards."}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "1. The role of financial intermediaries in the economy"}, {"u": 12, "k": "material", "x": "In today's business world, financial intermediaries play a vital role in fostering the economic growth and maintaining the stability of the financial system. First of all, financial intermediaries are financial institutions that help tranfer funds from people who have surplus money those who need to borrow y. In other words, they are third parties that connect savers and borrowers. In is noticeable that most commercial banks in the economy are financial intermediaries. Banks play a really vital role in supporting the economic growth as they can lend money to businesses so that businesses will have funds to continue and expand their business operation. When businesses can expand their business, the economy will develop quickly. On the other hand, financial intermediaries help people who have surplus fund earn interest based on depositing their money with financial intermediaries. Financial intermediaries have strong impact on the economy as they can adjust their interst as much as they want to influence the economy. They can lower their interest rates in order to support businesses during economic crisis such as the Ferderal Severse in the US in the world economic crisis in 2008. These actions helped businesses borrowed money with lower interest rates, so they can have more money to continue their operation. Furthermore, reducing interest rate also encouraged people to spend more and borrow more. It is clear that financial intermediaries not only play a role of a third party to connect people who want to earn interest on savings to people who have the need to borrow money, but also have a strong influence in the money supply of the economy."}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "2. Different types of banks and financial institutions"}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "3. The differences between banks and non-bank financial intermediaries"}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "4. the role of commercial banks in providing financial services"}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "5. Islamic banking and conventional banking"}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "6. The importance of the banking system in morden society"}, {"u": 12, "k": "prompt", "x": "7. The risk faced by banks and their impact on the economy"}], "tfng": [{"t": "Manufacturing companies require three basic functions: finance, production or operations, and marketing. Operations management is also of crucial importance to service companies. The objectives of the production department are usually to produce a specific product, on schedule, at minimum cost. Most companies have to choose between price, quality and flexibility. Workers will often treat the public the same way that management treats them. Companies must decide how much R&D to do themselves, or use research institutes or license designs from others. Introducing new products obviously requires accurate sales forecasting.", "q": [["Operations management is important to all businesses.", "T"], ["Production departments usually concentrate on quality, quantity and flexibility.", "F"], ["Workers who are treated well will probably be more productive.", "NG"], ["Large companies are obliged to do their own R&D.", "F"], ["Decisions about new products depend on sales forecasting.", "T"]]}, {"t": "Traditionally organizations have a pyramidal structure with a clear chain of command. Everyone knows their line manager. Yet most activities are too complicated for a single hierarchy, so large manufacturers have a functional structure with specialist departments. A disadvantage is that people care more about their own department than the whole company, causing conflicts between finance and marketing, or marketing and production.", "q": [["Pyramid structures have a clear chain of command.", "T"], ["Functional departments always cooperate well.", "F"], ["Matrix management is the best structure for all firms.", "NG"], ["Marketing and production may disagree about objectives.", "T"], ["Functional structures were invented in Japan.", "NG"]]}], "qa": [["Why do some companies introduce multiple branding strategies?", "Different brands target different segments; protect the main brand if one fails; fill shelf space and reduce competitors’ room; avoid cannibalizing/ associating a low-end product with the premium name."], ["What are the objectives of a production department?", "Make the product on schedule at minimum cost; also quality and reliability, maximum output, full use of plant and workforce, short lead time, return on assets, flexibility. Some conflict, e.g. low cost vs quality."], ["Compare pyramid structure and functional structure.", "Pyramid: single chain of command, clear line authority, simple but slow and rigid. Functional: grouped by specialist departments, expertise and efficiency, but departmental conflicts and slower cross-department decisions."], ["What are the possible consequences of excess capacity?", "Idle machines and workers, higher unit costs, lower return on assets, price cuts, redundancies or plant closure; can be reduced by outsourcing, new products or selling spare capacity."], ["Distinguish quality control from quality assurance.", "QC inspects output to find defects (reactive); QA designs the process to prevent defects (proactive); TQM involves everyone."], ["What is the difference between a manager and a leader?", "Managers have subordinates, formal authority, and focus on the bottom line; leaders have followers by choice, inspire and look at the horizon."], ["Explain fiscal and monetary policy.", "Fiscal: taxation and public spending by government. Monetary: central bank controls money supply and interest rates. Both can boost or cool the economy."], ["Why do banks act as financial intermediaries?", "They take deposits from savers and lend to borrowers, transforming small deposits into loans, spreading risk and supporting growth."]], "essay": [["Why is it not usually possible to organize a large organization in a single hierarchy? Give examples of conflicts of interest between departments. Are there other ways of organizing companies?", "Intro: pyramid = clear chain of command. Body 1: too complex, so functional departments (production, finance, marketing, HR). Body 2: conflicts: finance wants low costs vs marketing wants big budgets; marketing wants variety vs production wants long runs; sales promises short lead times vs production. Body 3: solutions: matrix management, team-based structures, flattening hierarchies, delegation/decentralization, divisional structure. Conclusion: no perfect structure; combine. (≥250 words)"], ["Discuss the role of financial intermediaries in the economy.", "Define (connect savers and borrowers: banks, insurers, pension funds). Roles: channel funds, lend to businesses for growth, pay interest to savers, central banks influence via interest rates (e.g. 2008 crisis). Risks: credit crunch, default. Conclusion."], ["Can efficiency and employment be reconciled? Discuss downsizing and rationalization.", "Efficiency = no waste; firms restructure, downsize, delocalize to cut cost. Costs: job insecurity, morale, social cost. Mitigation: redundancy packages, retraining, employability. Conclude with balance and CSR."], ["Discuss advantages and disadvantages of free trade vs protectionism.", "Free trade: comparative advantage, lower prices, choice, efficiency. Protectionism (tariffs, quotas): protects infant and strategic industries and jobs, but raises prices and risks retaliation. Conclusion."]]};